    "description": "Iran's basic-science landscape includes mathematics, physics, chemistry, biological sciences, and Earth sciences across universities and specialist research institutions. Institutional examples show connections between fundamental questions, experimental research, and the engineering needed to build scientific instruments.",
    "tags": ["Basic sciences", "Mathematics", "Physics", "Chemistry", "Biological sciences", "Research"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T17:26:51Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T17:26:51Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Basic Sciences in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore Iran's basic-science disciplines through university departments, specialist institutions, and documented particle-physics research."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "institutional-landscape",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A family of disciplines",
        "content": "Basic sciences in Iran are distributed across institutions with different academic structures. The International Association of Universities' directory entry for Shahid Beheshti University, for example, lists mathematical sciences, physics, Earth sciences, chemistry and petroleum sciences, and life sciences and biotechnology. These names reveal an important distinction: a university's administrative boundaries do not necessarily separate fundamental inquiry from applied subjects. Chemistry can share a faculty with petroleum studies, while mathematics can sit alongside statistics and computer science. The directory is a historical institutional snapshot, updated in 2019, rather than confirmation of the university's complete structure at the research cutoff. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "zanjan-example",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A specialist institution outside Tehran",
        "content": "The Institute for Advanced Studies in Basic Sciences, usually abbreviated IASBS, provides a geographically distinct example in Zanjan. Its Uni24k directory entry lists biological sciences, chemistry, Earth sciences, information technology, mathematics, and physics. The fields recorded include cell and molecular biology, biochemistry, organic and analytical chemistry, geophysics, applied mathematics, and astronomy. [s2]\n\nThis grouping helps explain the breadth of the category. Basic science encompasses studies of living systems and the Earth as well as mathematical and physical questions. It also includes fields with explicit applied dimensions. The directory lists bachelor's, master's, and doctoral qualifications at institutional level, but does not establish that every department offers every qualification. Direct inspection of IASBS's own website was unsuccessful during this research, so current departmental offerings and admissions arrangements remain unverified. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "fundamental-research",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From theoretical questions to scientific instruments",
        "content": "A more detailed research example comes from the Institute for Research in Fundamental Sciences, known as IPM. Its profile on the International Particle Physics Outreach Group website describes work spanning astronomy, mathematics, biological sciences, physics, and other subjects. Within its School of Particles and Accelerators, the profile distinguishes experimental high-energy physics, theoretical high-energy physics, and accelerator research. [s3]\n\nThe experimental description identifies involvement with CMS, a particle detector experiment at CERN's Large Hadron Collider. The theoretical description includes quantum field theory and particle cosmology, while the accelerator group covers components and systems such as magnets, radio-frequency equipment, vacuum technology, and controls. Fundamental research therefore appears here alongside the technical work required to conduct experiments. These are institutional descriptions of research activity; they do not establish a national ranking or independently measure scientific impact. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reading-the-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "How to read the available picture",
        "content": "Together, these examples offer a useful starting map: comprehensive universities house several science disciplines, a specialist institution in Zanjan concentrates on basic sciences, and a Tehran research institute describes focused experimental and theoretical work. They should not be read as a complete inventory of Iranian science. Department lists establish subject coverage, while research-group descriptions establish stated activities; neither alone demonstrates laboratory access, teaching quality, graduate employment, or the distribution of research resources across the country. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
