    "description": "Management and economics occupy related but distinct institutional spaces in Iranian higher education. University examples connect management with business, public administration, accounting, and finance, while economics may be organized independently or alongside political science.",
    "tags": ["Management", "Economics", "Business education", "Accounting", "Public administration", "Universities"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T17:26:51Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T17:26:51Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Management and Economics in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Discover how Iranian universities organize economics, management, accounting, finance, and public administration, with historical context."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "disciplinary-map",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Related subjects, different academic homes",
        "content": "Management and economics should not be assumed to form a single university department in Iran. The University of Tehran maintains a separate economics faculty website and a management college website. The latter identifies specialist areas including industrial and technology management, business management, public administration and governance, accounting and finance, and entrepreneurship. This organization presents management as a broad field extending beyond private business. [s1][s2]\n\nAnother arrangement appears in the World Higher Education Database's Shahid Beheshti University entry. Economics is grouped with political science, while management and accountancy form a different faculty covering subjects such as finance, industrial management, business administration, and public administration. Because that entry was last updated in 2019, it is useful evidence of institutional organization at that time rather than a verified current prospectus. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "management-history",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "An administrative training institution becomes a faculty",
        "content": "The University of Tehran's own history traces its management college to an Institute of Administrative Sciences established in Solar Hijri year 1333 through an agreement with the University of Southern California. The institute initially operated within the Faculty of Law and Political Science and aimed to train administrators for public and private organizations. Its initial postgraduate subjects were administrative sciences and accounting; business administration followed. These are the university's historical claims, rather than an independently audited account of educational outcomes. [s4]\n\nThe same history records elevation to faculty status in Solar Hijri year 1343 and separation from law and political science. It later describes expansion into industrial management, finance and insurance, information technology management, and entrepreneurship. The original Iranian year labels are retained here: a Solar Hijri year overlaps parts of two Gregorian years, so replacing it with a single Gregorian date would imply unwarranted precision. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "economics-research",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Economics as a research field",
        "content": "The economics faculty's homepage describes its mission in terms of research addressing Iran's economic challenges. Its library notices include a book about econometric methods and causality, providing a specific example of engagement with the problem of identifying cause and effect in economic research. These notices establish published academic activity, rather than demonstrating agreement on a particular policy or the effectiveness of any proposed solution. [s1]\n\nInstitutional placement also helps explain the field's connections. An economics faculty can stand independently, as at Tehran, or appear alongside political science, as in the older Shahid Beheshti directory entry. Neither arrangement means that economics and management are interchangeable subjects. The distinction matters when reading university profiles or comparing the scope of programs. [s1][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "structure-and-outcomes",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What institutional growth does—and does not—show",
        "content": "Tehran's management website also lists a business research laboratory, research groups concerning sustainability and digital business, an innovation center, and an office for industry relations. These names document the college's stated organizational links between academic work and business practice. They do not establish how many students participate, how much external research is completed, or whether graduates obtain particular jobs. [s2]\n\nThe university's history describes repeated reorganizations, including changes to faculty and department status. Consequently, counting administrative units would be a poor substitute for measuring educational capacity or quality. A fuller national account requires dated curricula, comparable enrollment statistics, and independent evidence of graduate outcomes across institutions and regions. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s4"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
